== 1. Theorem 1 (sharp form) on random finite classes, majority decoder ==
S= 12 n=  6 |H|= 40 rho=0.34  adversary=False: E sum 1[B_t] =   0.077   n E[K]/(floor+1) =   14.46   E[K]/rho =   21.27
S= 12 n=  6 |H|= 40 rho=0.34  adversary=True : E sum 1[B_t] =   0.153   n E[K]/(floor+1) =   13.60   E[K]/rho =   20.00
S= 12 n=  6 |H|= 40 rho=0.0   adversary=False: E sum 1[B_t] =   2.267   n E[K]/(floor+1) =   42.14   E[K]/rho =     inf
S= 12 n=  6 |H|= 40 rho=0.0   adversary=True : E sum 1[B_t] =   2.933   n E[K]/(floor+1) =   42.22   E[K]/rho =     inf
S= 20 n= 10 |H|= 60 rho=0.25  adversary=False: E sum 1[B_t] =   0.197   n E[K]/(floor+1) =   26.74   E[K]/rho =   32.09
S= 20 n= 10 |H|= 60 rho=0.25  adversary=True : E sum 1[B_t] =   0.430   n E[K]/(floor+1) =   26.60   E[K]/rho =   31.92
S= 20 n= 10 |H|= 60 rho=0.5   adversary=False: E sum 1[B_t] =   0.093   n E[K]/(floor+1) =   13.15   E[K]/rho =   15.78
S= 20 n= 10 |H|= 60 rho=0.5   adversary=True : E sum 1[B_t] =   0.100   n E[K]/(floor+1) =   12.47   E[K]/rho =   14.96
largest ratio (observed / sharp bound): 0.069  (must be <= 1 up to noise)

== 2. Per-run posterior sampling vs Theorem 1 (K(P) = 1 bit, rho = 1/2) ==
n=   16 (m = n alternatives): per-run sampler E[bad rounds] =     5.97 (= 0.373 n);  majority decoder: 0;  per-step sampler: 0.000;  Theorem 1 bound K/rho = 2
n=   64 (m = n alternatives): per-run sampler E[bad rounds] =    23.63 (= 0.369 n);  majority decoder: 0;  per-step sampler: 0.000;  Theorem 1 bound K/rho = 2
n=  256 (m = n alternatives): per-run sampler E[bad rounds] =    94.26 (= 0.368 n);  majority decoder: 0;  per-step sampler: 0.000;  Theorem 1 bound K/rho = 2
n= 1024 (m = n alternatives): per-run sampler E[bad rounds] =   376.79 (= 0.368 n);  majority decoder: 0;  per-step sampler: 0.000;  Theorem 1 bound K/rho = 2

== 3. Proposition 2, second claim, exact enumeration with adaptive queries ==
200 random cases: max |Pr[imitator has Phi] - Pr[human has Phi]| = 1.11e-16 (adaptive queries, test problem fixed)
with the test problem chosen using the human: largest gap Pr[human] - Pr[imitator] = 0.078

== 4. Per-step error rate e versus rho-robustness ==
n = 1000, per-step error e = 0.001, rho = 0.001 (tolerates 1 replaced step): a mistake count of 2 with probability 0.50 (else 0) has per-step error 0.001 and run failure 0.50.
   Markov: failure of a rho-robust property <= e n / (floor(rho n)+1) = 0.5000 for rho = 0.001
   Markov: failure of a rho-robust property <= e n / (floor(rho n)+1) = 0.0909 for rho = 0.01
   Markov: failure of a rho-robust property <= e n / (floor(rho n)+1) = 0.0099 for rho = 0.1

== 5. Hidden-insight class: fewest replaced steps destroying 'ends with the right insight' ==
silent   n=8: arbitrary replacements: [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1] (by insight step t = 0..7); omission-only: [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1]
catch-up n=8: arbitrary replacements: [1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1] (by insight step t = 0..7); omission-only: [8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1]
   (catch-up with omissions: n - t replaced steps are needed, i.e. robust with a problem-dependent
    rho = (n - t - 1)/n against omissions only; against arbitrary replacements one step always suffices)

